  "narrative": {
    "intro": "North Carolina federal contract obligations total $4.8B in FY2026 to date. North Carolina received 163,507 awards so far, and the average award size is $29.6K. North Carolina federal contract obligations are down 48.1% year over year. North Carolina saw the Department of Defense lead buying with $2B, followed by the General Services Administration with $726M and the Department of Veterans Affairs with $633M. North Carolina vendors leading receipts include RESEARCH TRIANGLE INSTITUTE with $287M and SDV OFFICE SYSTEMS LLC with $226M. North Carolina industries with the largest contract dollars are Computer Systems Design Services at $505M and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $353M. North Carolina obligations by fiscal year show a peak at $9.3B in FY25 and the current FY26 total of $4.8B. North Carolina contract counts and dollar totals are partial for the fiscal year to date, so the FY2026 total is not a full-year figure.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest buyers in North Carolina right now?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $2B in obligations. The General Services Administration follows with $726M and the Department of Veterans Affairs follows with $633M. The Department of Health and Human Services and the Department of Homeland Security round out the top five with $438M and $338M respectively."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which contractors and industries dominate North Carolina federal spending?",
        "body": "RESEARCH TRIANGLE INSTITUTE is the top vendor at $287M, followed by SDV OFFICE SYSTEMS LLC at $226M and GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. at $186M. Computer Systems Design Services leads industries with $505M, and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction follows with $353M. Engineering Services and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) also rank high at $307M and $285M respectively."
      }
    ]
  },
